WEBVTT

00:00:00.389 --> 00:00:02.555
A glossary term does two jobs.

00:00:02.555 --> 00:00:04.071
It gets its own page.

00:00:04.071 --> 00:00:13.456
And wherever the term appears in an FAQ or a video's notes, the site turns it into a link with a pop-up definition, automatically.

00:00:13.456 --> 00:00:18.149
In this video, we'll add a practice term, and see it link itself.

00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:21.845
Open Glossary, and click New Term.

00:00:21.845 --> 00:00:24.985
Type the term as it should appear.

00:00:25.648 --> 00:00:29.987
The short definition shows in the pop-up, and in the glossary list.

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:36.528
It has a limit of two hundred and eighty characters, and anything longer is refused when you publish.

00:00:37.086 --> 00:00:40.206
So keep it to one or two lines.

00:00:43.538 --> 00:00:47.053
Aliases are the other ways the term is written.

00:00:47.053 --> 00:00:48.773
Click Add for each one.

00:00:48.773 --> 00:00:50.418
Capitals don't matter.

00:00:51.132 --> 00:00:53.225
One rule matters a lot here.

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:55.991
An alias can belong to only one term.

00:00:55.991 --> 00:01:04.215
If two terms share a word, the site can't be rebuilt, and nobody's changes go live until the clash is removed.

00:01:04.215 --> 00:01:08.252
So before adding an alias, search the glossary for it.

00:01:08.812 --> 00:01:15.132
Related terms, FAQs and videos are hand-picked links for the term's own page.

00:01:17.699 --> 00:01:20.594
Recommended reading works the same way.

00:01:20.594 --> 00:01:25.939
Click Add, then fill in the title, the full web address, and the author.

00:01:26.587 --> 00:01:32.147
The long explanation is optional, and shows only on the term's own page.

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:34.578
Publish now.

00:01:39.614 --> 00:01:48.774
This is the term's page, with its definition, explanation, related links and reading list, and the FAQs that mention it.

00:01:49.482 --> 00:01:52.679
And here's an FAQ that mentions absenteeism.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.820
The site has turned the first mention into a dotted link.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.162
Point at it, and the short definition pops up.

00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:07.113
Only the first mention on each page becomes a link, so pages don't fill up with underlines.

00:02:07.113 --> 00:02:11.569
Mentions inside headings, existing links and code are left alone.

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:14.037
And draft terms don't link anywhere.

00:02:14.037 --> 00:02:18.835
The handbook explains how to stop one particular mention from linking.

00:02:19.544 --> 00:02:22.624
Next: moderating reader comments.
